## 2026-09-30T12:22:00+05:30 corrects C2 — the plan's "all 256 byte chars present" is
### NOT the invariant for this checkpoint, and the port was wrong before the gate ran
This checkpoint is a byte-FALLBACK BPE over RAW CHARACTERS, not a GPT-2 byte-level
BPE. Evidence, all from its own tokenizer.json:
  - decoder = Sequence[ Replace(U+2581->" "), ByteFallback, Fuse ] -- NO ByteLevel.
    The decoder is what inverts the alphabet, so it is the discriminator.
  - ' ' (U+0020) is NOT a vocab entry. In a bytes_to_unicode alphabet the space
    would be U+0120; here it is absent, so a space MUST be rewritten before it can
    tokenize -- which is exactly what the Replace normalizer does.
  - vocab has U+2581=235248, 'hello'=17534, U+2581+h=531, U+2581hello=25612. A
    GPT-2 byte-level vocab contains NONE of these (it would write U+2581 as the
    three stand-ins for E2 96 81).
  - merges are over RAW chars.
  - 255 of the 256 <0xNN> pieces present; <0x09> missing because tab is id 226.
THE TRAP: this 256k vocab HAPPENS to contain all 256 GPT-2 byte chars, so the old
"byte characters all present" check PASSES for it and a runtime that maps bytes
anyway looks right. First version of the port did exactly that: 'hello world' came
back as 8 ids where HF gives 2. Fixed by making byte_mode a TABLE FIELD read from
the decoder, and branching the alphabet-closedness check with it.
Fixes to the plan's Phase 2 list: byte_fallback is NOT inert (it is the live
mechanism); unk_token is real (<unk>=3, fuse_unk true); and the pre-tokenizer order
is the existing one (see C1). Blob v2 APPENDS so v1 stays readable.

